The <strong>Service Container</strong> is Laravel's dependency injection container and object management system. It is responsible for creating and resolving class objects automatically.<br><br>We register classes or interfaces in the Service Container using <code>bind()</code> or <code>singleton()</code> inside a Service Provider's <code>register()</code> method. Laravel automatically injects those dependencies into a class through constructor injection or method injection.<br><br><pre><code>// Binding in a Service Provider
$this->app->bind(PaymentGateway::class, StripeGateway::class);
$this->app->singleton(Logger::class, function ($app) {
    return new FileLogger(storage_path('logs/laravel.log'));
});</code></pre><br>Then Laravel auto-resolves via type-hinting:<br><pre><code>class OrderController {
    public function __construct(
        protected PaymentGateway $gateway
    ) {}
}</code></pre>

A <strong>transaction</strong> is a group of SQL operations that are treated as a single unit of work. If all queries succeed, the transaction is committed. If any query fails, all previous changes are rolled back. This keeps the database consistent.<br><br>In Laravel, transactions are used like this:<br><pre><code>use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;

DB::beginTransaction();

try {
    User::create(['name' => 'John']);
    Order::create(['user_id' => 1, 'amount' => 100]);

    DB::commit();
} catch (\Exception $e) {
    DB::rollBack();
    throw $e;
}</code></pre><br>Alternatively use the closure form: <code>DB::transaction(function () { ... })</code>.
